    "transcript": [
      {
        "role": "H1",
        "text": "Saving, Investment, and National-Saving Identities",
        "node": 0
      },
      {
        "role": "P",
        "text": "MACROECONOMICS",
        "node": 1
      },
      {
        "role": "P",
        "text": "MACRO-42",
        "node": 2
      },
      {
        "role": "P",
        "text": "Time: 6 minutes",
        "node": 3
      },
      {
        "role": "P",
        "text": "Outcome: Calculate private, public, and national saving in closed and open economies.",
        "node": 4
      },
      {
        "role": "P",
        "text": "Difficulty: Intermediate",
        "node": 5
      },
      {
        "role": "H2",
        "text": "THE CORE IDEA",
        "node": 6
      },
      {
        "role": "P",
        "text": "With net taxes T, private saving is Y - T - C and public saving is T - G. National saving is their sum: S = Y - C - G. In a closed economy, S = I. In an open economy, S = I + NCO; because NX = NCO in the course model, S = I + NX. These are aggregate identities, not claims that each saver directly funds one investor.",
        "node": 7
      },
      {
        "role": "H2",
        "text": "HOW TO RECOGNIZE IT",
        "node": 8
      },
      {
        "role": "Lbl",
        "text": "-",
        "node": 11
      },
      {
        "role": "LBody",
        "text": "Budget surplus means T - G is positive; a deficit makes public saving negative.",
        "node": 12
      },
      {
        "role": "Lbl",
        "text": "-",
        "node": 14
      },
      {
        "role": "LBody",
        "text": "Adding private and public saving cancels T and gives Y - C - G.",
        "node": 15
      },
      {
        "role": "Lbl",
        "text": "-",
        "node": 17
      },
      {
        "role": "LBody",
        "text": "Use S = I only for the closed-economy model.",
        "node": 18
      },
      {
        "role": "Lbl",
        "text": "-",
        "node": 20
      },
      {
        "role": "LBody",
        "text": "In an open economy, saving can finance domestic investment or net capital outflow.",
        "node": 21
      },
      {
        "role": "H2",
        "text": "WATCH OUT",
        "node": 22
      },
      {
        "role": "P",
        "text": "Do not reverse the public-saving sign. T - G is public saving; G - T is the budget deficit. Also state the closed-economy assumption before writing S = I.",
        "node": 23
      },
      {
        "role": "H2",
        "text": "WORKED EXAMPLE: FROM COMPONENTS TO OPEN-ECONOMY SAVING",
        "node": 24
      },
      {
        "role": "H3",
        "text": "KEY RELATIONSHIPS",
        "node": 25
      },
      {
        "role": "Formula",
        "text": "Private saving = Y - T - C",
        "node": 26
      },
      {
        "role": "Formula",
        "text": "Public saving = T - G",
        "node": 27
      },
      {
        "role": "Formula",
        "text": "National saving = Y - C - G",
        "node": 28
      },
      {
        "role": "Formula",
        "text": "Closed: S = I",
        "node": 29
      },
      {
        "role": "Formula",
        "text": "Open: S = I + NCO; NX = NCO",
        "node": 30
      },
      {
        "role": "P",
        "text": "Let Y = $1,200, T = $150, C = $760, and G = $260. Private saving is $290; public saving is -$110; national saving is $180. If domestic investment is $140, then NCO = S - I = $40. The accounting identity also gives NX = $40.",
        "node": 31
      },
      {
        "role": "H2",
        "text": "CHECK YOURSELF",
        "node": 32
      },
      {
        "role": "P",
        "text": "Private saving is $220 and public saving is -$50. What is national saving?",
        "node": 33
      },
      {
        "role": "H2",
        "text": "READY?",
        "node": 34
      },
      {
        "role": "P",
        "text": "Return to the game and master this concept.",
        "node": 35
      }
    ],
