    "inlineFormulaSemantics": "SOURCE_BOUND_RUNS",
    "transcript": [
      {
        "role": "H1",
        "text": "Capital Flows and Net Capital Outflow",
        "node": 0
      },
      {
        "role": "P",
        "text": "MACROECONOMICS",
        "node": 1
      },
      {
        "role": "P",
        "text": "MACRO-55",
        "node": 2
      },
      {
        "role": "P",
        "text": "Time: 5 minutes",
        "node": 3
      },
      {
        "role": "P",
        "text": "Outcome: Classify international asset purchases and trace interest-rate effects on NCO.",
        "node": 4
      },
      {
        "role": "P",
        "text": "Difficulty: Intermediate",
        "node": 5
      },
      {
        "role": "H2",
        "text": "THE CORE IDEA",
        "node": 6
      },
      {
        "role": "P",
        "text": "Net capital outflow equals domestic residents' purchases of foreign assets minus foreign residents' purchases of domestic assets. Positive NCO is net capital outflow; negative NCO is net capital inflow. Capital tends to move toward more attractive risk-adjusted returns. In the textbook model, a higher domestic real interest rate lowers NCO and therefore reduces the supply of dollars in the foreign-exchange market.",
        "node": 7
      },
      {
        "role": "H2",
        "text": "HOW TO RECOGNIZE IT",
        "node": 8
      },
      {
        "role": "Lbl",
        "text": "-",
        "node": 11
      },
      {
        "role": "LBody",
        "text": "A U.S. resident buying a foreign bond raises U.S. NCO.",
        "node": 12
      },
      {
        "role": "Lbl",
        "text": "-",
        "node": 14
      },
      {
        "role": "LBody",
        "text": "A foreign resident buying a U.S. asset lowers U.S. NCO.",
        "node": 15
      },
      {
        "role": "Lbl",
        "text": "-",
        "node": 17
      },
      {
        "role": "LBody",
        "text": "Higher domestic real returns attract inflow and discourage outflow, lowering NCO.",
        "node": 18
      },
      {
        "role": "Lbl",
        "text": "-",
        "node": 20
      },
      {
        "role": "LBody",
        "text": "NCO supplies dollars to the FX market as residents acquire foreign assets.",
        "node": 21
      },
      {
        "role": "H2",
        "text": "WATCH OUT",
        "node": 22
      },
      {
        "role": "P",
        "text": "NCO and NX describe different transactions: assets versus goods and services. They are equal as an accounting relationship in the course model, not because every asset purchase is an export.",
        "node": 23
      },
      {
        "role": "H2",
        "text": "WORKED EXAMPLE: CLASSIFY, THEN NET",
        "node": 24
      },
      {
        "role": "H3",
        "text": "KEY RELATIONSHIPS",
        "node": 25
      },
      {
        "role": "LBody",
        "text": "NCO = domestic purchases of foreign assets - foreign purchases of domestic assets",
        "node": 28
      },
      {
        "role": "LBody",
        "text": "Positive NCO = net outflow",
        "node": 30
      },
      {
        "role": "LBody",
        "text": "Higher domestic real rate -> lower NCO",
        "node": 32
      },
      {
        "role": "LBody",
        "text": "In the course model: NCO = NX",
        "node": 34
      },
      {
        "role": "P",
        "text": "U.S. residents buy $140 billion of foreign assets; foreigners buy $90 billion of U.S. assets. U.S. NCO is $140 - $90 = +$50 billion, a net outflow. That positive NCO supplies dollars in exchange for foreign currency and corresponds to NX of +$50 billion.",
        "node": 35
      },
      {
        "role": "H2",
        "text": "CHECK YOURSELF",
        "node": 36
      },
      {
        "role": "P",
        "text": "Foreign purchases of U.S. assets rise while U.S. foreign-asset purchases are fixed. What happens to U.S. NCO?",
        "node": 37
      },
      {
        "role": "H2",
        "text": "READY?",
        "node": 38
      },
      {
        "role": "P",
        "text": "Return to the game and master this concept.",
        "node": 39
      }
    ],
